the buildup of forces uh is really quite  significant and yesterday uh russia  began the military exercises in belarus  that are due to the last 10 days and  that's 30 000 troops on the move  they've also put fighter aircraft into  belarus and they closed off large  swathes of the black sea and closed off  the sea of azov  so they've really escalated dramatically  uh the capacity that russia has that is  ready to move and what they can bring to  bear to the conflict on top of that one  has to speculate that maybe there's  something in signals intelligence that  the u.s has heard we don't know that for  a fact it would be classified  but the the degree of certainty with  which the u.s is addressing this now  makes one suspect that they have heard  something and i would just add that that  still doesn't mean that we know for sure  uh putin can decide to intervene or  decide not to and i think we may still  have an opportunity to try to persuade  him not to  yeah what came across for me certainly  was that real sense of urgency  especially when he said to american  citizens repeating that plea you need to  get out within the next 24 or 48 hours  and quite frankly we won't rescue you if  you choose to stay and this evening our  foreign office here in the uk has said  uh to british citizens as well that they  should consider uh leaving um  what is in putin's interest in invading  uh  what do you think is is going through  his mind in terms of what he could  actually win by carrying out an invasion  well i think there are several different  ways to look at that  one of them is to be building a greater  russia he wants to be one of the few  russian leaders who accumulated the  lands and who built a greater russia so  taking territory uh from ukraine where  russian speakers live i think that is  itself part of an objective he's done it  already with crimea and the eastern part  of donbass but he may like to have more  uh he also remember has taken over the  security services in belarus and in  kazakhstan and taken pieces of georgia  and moldova so this fits a pattern in  addition to that he's trying to rewrite  the european security architecture  since the 1970s we've had the helsinki  accords which have said that we respect  every nation in europe sovereignty and  territorial integrity and its right to  make its own choices about its security  and its political orientation putin  wants to tear that up and go back to  yalta where stalin and  roosevelt and churchill sat together and  drew a line across europe and said the  eastern part here is russia's sphere of  influence the rest of it is a western  sphere that's what putin really wants is  a recognition that  ukraine georgia any former soviet  territory parts of central and eastern  europe those are his and uh he's not  going to get that so he's going to try  to then carve out some of that by force  if he uses force but there is an  argument isn't there that he's in a far  stronger position by threatening and  bullying and intimidating rather than  invading becoming the world's pariah and  also having to take lots of dead russian  soldiers back to their mothers in body  bags  indeed and i think that that's a very  important point both uh  if the russian public sees that he is  killing ukrainians russian soldiers are  there dying as well it's  incontrovertibly russia that is the  aggressor here that will not get a lot  of understanding in moscow no matter how  much  the kremlin controls the media there  people will know and people won't  understand so that's one dynamic and the  other is you're exactly right by  building up the threats and making  demands like this he's already made some  achievements here that he can walk away  with  he's gotten all the world focused on him  he's had the u.s offer bilateral  meetings and arms control nuclear arms  control  exercise and transparency and confidence  building measures uh he's had macron  probably signal to him that france would  not support ukrainian nato membership so  he's probably accumulating a lot of that  already without invading uh we'll we  don't know whether he actually will  invade he certainly can and the forces  are there and ready but uh  i think what we need to do is focus on  those costs if we can make it guaranteed  that russia would face unacceptable  military costs if it invades ukraine  again um that is the best chance for  getting him not to do it  yeah so uh uh we've just got a minute  left but what is your assessment then  given that news conference given the  intense diplomacy that's been going on  over the last 24 48 hours what's your  assessment of of what happens next it's  a tricky question i know  yeah i know what what i think happens  next is the the west is drawing down a  bit i wish that what we were doing was  ramping up our support for ukraine more  security assistance more creative ways  of helping them maybe even some presence  of nato advisors uh really show that  this would be costly for putin i don't  know if we'll do that but i think that's  the right next step  yeah um uh of course the the problem in  all of this is nobody can read uh  putin's mind  yeah no that's right and uh he's he's  put in place the forces now so he's the  one who um can take the initiative or  not  okay uh kurt volcker former united  states permanent representative to nato  also served as u.s special  representative for ukraine negotiations  in 2017 and 2019. we appreciate your  analysis thank you very much indeed for  joining us
 

